      "title": "Overlooked trap awaits Trump DOJ officials fleeing department: ex-prosecutor",
      "description": "Former federal prosecutor Glenn Kirschner says departing Justice Department officials under President Donald Trump could still be impeached and barred from future government service \u2014 even after fleeing the job. In a Substack Live with All Rise News\u2019 Adam Klasfeld, Kirschner discussed the exits of Civil Rights Division head Harmeet Dhillon; Jason Reding Qui\u00f1ones, the U.S. attorney in Miami who led the \u201cgrand conspiracy\u201d probe; and National Security Division chief John Eisenberg. Klasfeld attributed the departures to an incoming Democratic Congress with subpoena power, noting Rep. Jamie Raskin is already examining the SPLC prosecution. \u201cWinter is coming,\u201d Klasfeld said. Kirschner said resignation doesn\u2019t end accountability. He listed congressional investigations, bar complaints and possible criminal probes, though he said the latter would likely wait until DOJ has \u201chonest, ethical, law-abiding leadership,\u201d probably January 2029. He compared the officials\u2019 fate to Trump\u2019s first group of lawyers who challenged the 2020 results, several of whom were disbarred. Then he raised impeachment. People may assume resignation makes it moot, Kirschner said, but a Senate conviction can also impose disqualification from future government service. He cited the 1876 case of War Secretary William Belknap, who resigned as the House moved ahead, only for the Senate to hold a trial anyway. Belknap was ultimately acquitted when the vote fell short of the two-thirds needed to convict. Kirschner said he wants every Trump official with enough evidence against them impeached, in office or not, so they would \u201cnever be permitted to return to government in the future.\u201d Klasfeld noted the recent precedent of Trump\u2019s Senate trial after he left office, which Senate Republicans used as a procedural escape hatch. He also pointed to U.S. District Judge David O. Carter, who last week rejected a Justice Department bid to set aside the ballots of more than 20,000 California voters and described a \u201cbroad, ongoing pattern of attempted election interference.\u201d Klasfeld cited Dhillon\u2019s voter-roll lawsuits as a prime example. Dismantling Trump\u2019s Department of Revenge by Adam Klasfeld The senior DOJ officials are leaving at a furious pace, but they may be jumping from the frying pan into the fire. Read on Substack",
